# >>> ACC surface — IGNORED. Basis: commit 0db2a19 (2026-08-20). >>>
#
# "This repo is PUBLIC and holds only the website, so the scope's artifacts use the
# gitignored-in-tree class": OneDrive syncs and backs them up, git does not publish them. Of the
# workspace's exclusion rules this is the only one guarding a LIVE public remote — the other repos
# with the same posture have no remote configured at all — so this is the one where a mistaken
# `git add` actually publishes.
#
# Search still reaches these files — see _Projects\GitHub\.ignore, which ripgrep and fd read and
# which outranks .gitignore.
#
# IGNORE THE WHOLE SURFACE, NOT THE FOLDERS KNOWN WHEN THE RULE WAS WRITTEN. This rule named
# `.aia/.artifacts/` only, and ACC later wrote `.aia/focus-proposals.ndjson` beside it — untracked
# AND unignored in a public repo, one `git add -A` away from publishing working state (caught
# 2026-09-12). Nothing under `.aia/` is part of the website, so cover the next file ACC invents
# before it exists rather than after. Six other repos still have that exact exposure.
#
# `.artifacts/` is kept alongside it: with no leading slash it matches at ANY depth, which is why it
# survived the August 2026 move to `.aia/.artifacts/` untouched. Never write `/.artifacts/` — the
# leading slash pins the rule to the repo root and it silently stops matching the day the folder
# moves — and never rely on a `!` negation under a `*/` blanket. Every gitignore breakage observed
# in this workspace has been one of those two shapes.
.aia/
.artifacts/
.changelog.md
